TASK 2 — Configure HTTPS
This is an important part of your assessment.
Harbor's official documentation recommends HTTPS for production/non-air-gapped environments. �
Harbor
For this lab, we'll create a self-signed certificate.
Step 6 — Decide Your Harbor Hostname
Let's use:
harbor.local
Your harbor.yml should eventually contain:
hostname: harbor.local
Find this section:
hostname: reg.mydomain.com
Change it to:
hostname: harbor.local
Step 7 — Configure Harbor Admin Password
Find:
harbor_admin_password:
Set:
harbor_admin_password: Harbor@123
You can use another strong password.
Your first login will be:
Username: admin
Password: Harbor@123
Step 8 — Configure HTTPS
In harbor.yml, find the HTTPS section:
https:
  port: 443
  certificate: /your/certificate/path
  private_key: /your/private/key/path
We'll use:
https:
  port: 443
  certificate: /data/cert/harbor.local.crt
  private_key: /data/cert/harbor.local.key
Save the file.
In nano:
CTRL + O
ENTER
CTRL + X
Step 9 — Generate Certificate Authority
Create a certificate directory:
sudo mkdir -p /data/cert
Go there:
cd /data/cert
Generate CA private key:
sudo openssl genrsa -out ca.key 4096
Generate CA certificate:
sudo openssl req -x509 -new -nodes -sha512 -days 3650 \
-subj "/C=IN/ST=TamilNadu/L=Chennai/O=KIAQ/OU=DevOps/CN=Harbor-CA" \
-key ca.key \
-out ca.crt
Step 10 — Generate Harbor Private Key
sudo openssl genrsa -out harbor.local.key 4096
Step 11 — Create Certificate Signing Request
sudo openssl req -sha512 -new \
-subj "/C=IN/ST=TamilNadu/L=Chennai/O=KIAQ/OU=DevOps/CN=harbor.local" \
-key harbor.local.key \
-out harbor.local.csr
Step 12 — Create SAN Configuration
This step is important because modern clients validate the Subject Alternative Name.
Run:
sudo nano /data/cert/v3.ext
Add:
authorityKeyIdentifier=keyid,issuer
basicConstraints=CA:FALSE
keyUsage=digitalSignature,nonRepudiation,keyEncipherment,dataEncipherment
extendedKeyUsage=serverAuth

subjectAltName=@alt_names

[alt_names]
DNS.1=harbor.local
DNS.2=localhost
IP.1=192.168.1.100
Change this:
192.168.1.100
to your actual Harbor server IP.
Save:
CTRL + O
ENTER
CTRL + X
Step 13 — Generate Harbor Certificate
Run:
sudo openssl x509 -req -sha512 -days 3650 \
-extfile /data/cert/v3.ext \
-CA /data/cert/ca.crt \
-CAkey /data/cert/ca.key \
-CAcreateserial \
-in /data/cert/harbor.local.csr \
-out /data/cert/harbor.local.crt
Check:
ls -l /data/cert
You should have:
ca.crt
ca.key
ca.srl
harbor.local.crt
harbor.local.csr
harbor.local.key
v3.ext
Step 14 — Configure Your Ubuntu Machine to Trust the Certificate
Copy CA certificate:
sudo cp /data/cert/ca.crt /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/harbor-ca.crt
Update certificates:
sudo update-ca-certificates
Step 15 — Configure Docker to Trust Harbor
Create:
sudo mkdir -p /etc/docker/certs.d/harbor.local
Convert certificate:
sudo openssl x509 \
-in /data/cert/harbor.local.crt \
-out /etc/docker/certs.d/harbor.local/harbor.local.cert
Copy key:
sudo cp /data/cert/harbor.local.key \
/etc/docker/certs.d/harbor.local/harbor.local.key
Copy CA:
sudo cp /data/cert/ca.crt \
/etc/docker/certs.d/harbor.local/ca.crt
Harbor's documentation specifies this Docker certificate directory arrangement for Docker clients using a private CA. �
